<h1>CBH Fasteners — Mobile App & Website Unified Architecture</h1>

<div class="tagline">
  <strong>Technical Architecture & Communication Specification</strong><br/>
  <strong>Project:</strong> CBH Fasteners Industrial Platform (Desktop Web + Android Native App)<br/>
  <strong>Backend Engine:</strong> Next.js 14 REST API & MySQL 8.0 Relational Database<br/>
  <strong>Mobile Framework:</strong> React Native 0.84 (Direct Android Bridge)
</div>

<h2>1. Executive Summary: How Mobile & Web Communicate</h2>
<p>
  <strong>Yes, the mobile app and website use the exact same backend API and share the exact same database.</strong>
</p>
<p>
  There is <strong>no separate database</strong> and <strong>no duplicate backend</strong>. The CBH platform operates on a single unified architecture:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Unified Data Engine:</strong> When an Admin creates a new Fastener SKU in the Mobile App, it sends an HTTP POST request to <code>/api/products</code>. That API writes directly into the MySQL <code>products</code> table. Refreshing the website immediately displays the newly created product.</li>
  <li><strong>Instant Bi-Directional Sync:</strong> When a customer places an order on the website, it is stored in the MySQL <code>orders</code> table. Opening the Mobile App Admin Console immediately shows the new order in real-time.</li>
  <li><strong>Shared Business Logic:</strong> Price tiers (Bronze, Silver, Gold, VIP), volume discount calculations, ERPNext sync queues, and authentication credentials are identical across both platforms.</li>
</ul>

<h2>2. Communication Flow Diagram</h2>
<pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────┐     ┌──────────────────────────────────────┐
│        🌐 DESKTOP WEB STOREFRONT      │     │      📱 ANDROID NATIVE MOBILE APP     │
│       (Browser: localhost:3000)      │     │      (React Native: 10.0.2.2:3000)   │
└──────────────────┬───────────────────┘     └───────────────────┬──────────────────┘
                   │                                             │
                   │ HTTP Fetch (/api/...)                       │ HTTP apiFetch (/api/...)
                   ▼                                             ▼
┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                    NEXT.JS 14 UNIFIED REST API ENGINE (PORT 3000)                 │
│                                                                                   │
│  • POST /api/auth/login        -> Role-based authentication & bcrypt verification │
│  • GET, POST, PUT, DELETE /api/products -> Inventory catalog (605 warehouse SKUs) │
│  • GET, POST, PUT, DELETE /api/orders   -> Logistics & dispatch order pipeline    │
│  • GET, POST, PUT, DELETE /api/rfq      -> B2B bulk quotation inquiries           │
│  • GET, POST, PUT, DELETE /api/users    -> Corporate client registry & credit     │
│  • GET /api/erpnext/status              -> Real-time plant sync telemetry         │
└─────────────────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────────┘
                                          │
                                          │ Connection Pool (mysql2)
                                          ▼
┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                       🗄️ MYSQL RELATIONAL DATABASE (PORT 3306)                     │
│                        Database: cbh_fasteners                                    │
│                        Tables: products, orders, order_items, users, bulk_rfqs   │
└───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>

<h2>3. Network Configuration & IP Routing</h2>
<p>
  When developing on an Android emulator or testing on a physical mobile device, standard <code>localhost:3000</code> does not reach your computer because Android runs its own Linux operating system where <code>localhost</code> means the phone itself.
</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Environment</th>
      <th>Base URL</th>
      <th>Description</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Android Emulator</strong></td>
      <td><code>http://10.0.2.2:3000/api</code></td>
      <td>Android's built-in virtual loopback IP pointing directly to your host PC's <code>localhost:3000</code>.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Physical Device (Wi-Fi)</strong></td>
      <td><code>http://192.168.1.x:3000/api</code></td>
      <td>Host machine LAN IP address when phone is on the same local Wi-Fi router.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>ADB Port Reverse (USB)</strong></td>
      <td><code>http://localhost:3000/api</code></td>
      <td>Via <code>adb reverse tcp:3000 tcp:3000</code>, forwarding emulator port 3000 to PC.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Production Live Server</strong></td>
      <td><code>https://cbh.store/api</code></td>
      <td>Cloud production domain with SSL encryption.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h3>Code Implementation: <code>mobile/config/env.ts</code></h3>
<pre>
import { Platform } from 'react-native';

const LOCAL_IP = Platform.OS === 'android' ? '10.0.2.2' : 'localhost';
const PORT = '3000';

export const ENV = {
  DEV_API_URL: `http://${LOCAL_IP}:${PORT}/api`,
  LAN_API_URL: 'http://192.168.1.6:3000/api',
  PROD_API_URL: 'https://cbh.socialflymediatech.com/api',
  IS_PROD: false, // Set to true when generating production release APK
};

export const API_BASE_URL = ENV.IS_PROD ? ENV.PROD_API_URL : ENV.DEV_API_URL;
</pre>

